WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.200
If the movie is good enough the effects won't matter.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.860
You look back at the original King Kong,
and you can tell that's like a puppet,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.607
but the movie is so good, you don't care.

00:00:13.607 --> 00:00:17.800
You really feel for that damn puppet when it falls off
the Empire State Building in the end.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.120
A practical effect is anything
that primarily exists there on the set.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.900
The actors can interact with them and they have a real sort of gravity and feeling to them

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.460
that computer effects can often lack,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.540
kind of [like] the first big movie that had a lot of visual effects in it was the 1924 version of the movie,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:46.200
"The Thief of Bagdad", which has all these incredible creations and concoctions and on-screen tricks

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.360
that make it seems like there's actual magic going on when obviously there isn't.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.840
The height of practical effects for me 
is probably the late 70s and 80s.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:03.280
*music*

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:26.940
The best special effects are probably in the original Jurassic Park. That was the height of practical effects

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.480
and also kind of the start of computer effects.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.180
*shows diagram of a model of a raptor(?) used in the film*

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.040
And for the most part they still hold up in a way that a lot of other computer-generated effects of the air at Dome.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:49.320
I think the "trend-away" really was kinda in the late 90s. You look at something like the Matrix.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.320
It's definitely sort of this watershed 
where people were like

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.820
"Oh, we can use computers 
to make things look really cool."

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.670
And then Hollywood just overdid it.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.980
I don't think computer effects are all bad necessarily.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:04.060
But they certainly have sort of a feeling to them 
that they don't exist in any physical reality whatsoever

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.000
and that creates kind of a disconnection 
between the movie in the audience.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.620
Now in the Hobbit movies
where the story doesn't work as well.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.780
I found myself really distracted, 
especially in the most recent one

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:19.380
by these gigantic moments of battle that have no real feeling of, like, weight to them of the characters

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.420
like behaving as physical beings in a universe

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.920
in particular there's a scene where Legolas
runs up a crumbling staircase

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.640
as it's falling out for beneath him.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.460
It takes you out of the movie because it's so ridiculous.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.100
The primary argument for practical effects 
is a storytelling one.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.409
It forces you to zero in on what you really want to do.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:43.769
I think that practical effects necessarily limit you by what is physically capable for actors in a certain situation.

